#: src/advanced-features.md:1
msgid "Advanced features"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:3
msgid ""
"The features listed on this page fall outside the rest of the main "
"categories."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:5
msgid ""
"`#[cfg(doc)]`: Documenting platform-specific or feature-specific information"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:7
msgid ""
"For conditional compilation, Rustdoc treats your crate the same way the "
"compiler does. Only things from the host target are available (or from the "
"given `--target` if present), and everything else is \"filtered out\" from "
"the crate. This can cause problems if your crate is providing different "
"things on different targets and you want your documentation to reflect all "
"the available items you provide."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:13
msgid ""
"If you want to make sure an item is seen by Rustdoc regardless of what "
"platform it's targeting, you can apply `#[cfg(doc)]` to it. Rustdoc sets "
"this whenever it's building documentation, so anything that uses that flag "
"will make it into documentation it generates. To apply this to an item with "
"other `#[cfg]` filters on it, you can write something like "
"`#[cfg(any(windows, doc))]`. This will preserve the item either when built "
"normally on Windows, or when being documented anywhere."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:20
msgid "Please note that this `cfg` is not passed to doctests."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:22
msgid "Example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:25
msgid "/// Token struct that can only be used on Windows.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:27
msgid "/// Token struct that can only be used on Unix.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:33
msgid ""
"Here, the respective tokens can only be used by dependent crates on their "
"respective platforms, but they will both appear in documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:36
msgid "Interactions between platform-specific docs"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:38
msgid ""
"Rustdoc does not have a magic way to compile documentation 'as-if' you'd run "
"it once for each platform (such a magic wand has been called the ['holy "
"grail of rustdoc'](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/1998)). Instead, "
"it sees _all_ of your code at once, the same way the Rust compiler would if "
"you passed it `--cfg doc`. The main difference is that rustdoc doesn't run "
"all the compiler passes, meaning that some invalid code won't emit an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:46
msgid "Add aliases for an item in documentation search"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:48
msgid ""
"This feature allows you to add alias(es) to an item when using the `rustdoc` "
"search through the `doc(alias)` attribute. Example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:52 src/advanced-features.md:66
msgid "\"x\""
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:53 src/advanced-features.md:66
msgid "\"big\""
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:57
msgid ""
"Then, when looking for it through the `rustdoc` search, if you enter \"x\" "
"or \"big\", search will show the `BigX` struct first."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:60
msgid ""
"There are some limitations on the doc alias names though: they cannot "
"contain quotes (`'`, `\"`) or most whitespace. ASCII space is allowed if it "
"does not start or end the alias."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:63
msgid "You can add multiple aliases at the same time by using a list:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:70
msgid "Custom search engines"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:72
msgid ""
"If you find yourself often referencing online Rust docs you might enjoy "
"using a custom search engine. This allows you to use the navigation bar "
"directly to search a `rustdoc` website. Most browsers support this feature "
"by letting you define a URL template containing `%s` which will be "
"substituted for the search term. As an example, for the standard library you "
"could use this template:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:78
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/?search=%s\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:82
msgid ""
"Note that this will take you to a results page listing all matches. If you "
"want to navigate to the first result right away (which is often the best "
"match) use the following instead:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:85
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/?search=%s&go_to_first=true\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:89
msgid ""
"This URL adds the `go_to_first=true` query parameter which can be appended "
"to any `rustdoc` search URL to automatically go to the first result."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:92
msgid "`#[repr(...)]`: Documenting the representation of a type"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:94
msgid ""
"Generally, rustdoc only displays the representation of a given type if none "
"of its variants are `#[doc(hidden)]` and if all of its fields are public and "
"not `#[doc(hidden)]` since it's likely not meant to be considered part of "
"the public ABI otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:98
msgid ""
"Note that there's no way to overwrite that heuristic and force rustdoc to "
"show the representation regardless."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:101
msgid "`#[repr(transparent)]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:103
msgid ""
"You can read more about `#[repr(transparent)]` itself in the [Rust Reference]"
"(https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/type-layout.html#the-transparent-"
"representation) and in the [Rustonomicon](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nomicon/"
"other-reprs.html#reprtransparent)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:106
msgid ""
"Since this representation is only considered part of the public ABI if the "
"single field with non-trivial size or alignment is public and if the "
"documentation does not state otherwise, rustdoc helpfully displays the "
"attribute if and only if the non-1-ZST field is public and not "
"`#[doc(hidden)]` or – in case all fields are 1-ZST fields — at least one "
"field is public and not `#[doc(hidden)]`. The term _1-ZST_ refers to types "
"that are one-aligned and zero-sized."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:112
msgid ""
"It would seem that one can manually hide the attribute with "
"`#[cfg_attr(not(doc), repr(transparent))]` if one wishes to declare the "
"representation as private even if the non-1-ZST field is public. However, "
"due to [current limitations](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/114952), this method is not always guaranteed to work. Therefore, if "
"you would like to do so, you should always write that down in prose "
"independently of whether you use `cfg_attr` or not."
msgstr ""
